  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">2023 Libya Floods - contribution via ICRC</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Benghazi, Derna and several other cities in the north-eastern part of the country,</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 10 September, Storm Daniel tore across Libya and caused deadly flooding in Benghazi, Derna and several other cities in the north-eastern part of the country, some of which have been sites of armed conflict. Many neighborhoods were swept out to sea by the deluge &#x2013; which brought waves as high as seven meters &#x2013; while some remain submerged.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ICRC has responded swiftly by organizing relief assistance for the most affected families, particularly in Derna and its surrounding areas. It has teams standing ready to distribute, with the Libyan Red Crescent Society, food items to up to 1,800 households, and essential household items (e.g. mattresses, kitchenware) and hygiene supplies to around 6,200 households. The ICRC is also gearing up to support the health ministry with donations of medicines and other medical supplies for primary-health-care centres.
The ICRC is supporting the emergency response of the Libyan Red Crescent, particularly its efforts to manage human remains. It has donated 1,000 body bags and personal protective equipment to the Libyan Red Crescent and to the authorities.
The ICRC is closely monitoring the situation in the flood-stricken areas to refine its response according to the needs and the evolution of the circumstances. It is working with the Libyan Red Crescent, the International Federation and other Movement components present in Libya to plan a cohesive emergency response.</narrative>
  </description>
